    {
      "id": "2wwWfErL906iS-Wggt98D",
      "type": "frame",
      "x": -5764.064084571518,
      "y": 10019.195047667401,
      "width": 680,
      "height": 1808,
      "name": "Data Engineering — Senior-Level Best Practices",
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#868e96",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1452415494,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 888002146,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784655825374,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "nHHri2RBh6qjA21V38wcj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -5740.064084571518,
      "y": 10073.195047667401,
      "width": 632,
      "height": 1720,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "2wwWfErL906iS-Wggt98D",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2002075178,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1231003413,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784655825374,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Batch vs streaming: choose based on latency requirements and cost, not novelty — batch (hourly/daily) is simpler, cheaper, easier to debug; streaming (Kafka/Flink) only when sub-minute freshness is a real business requirement.\n- Idempotent pipeline design: every task should be safely re-runnable without duplicating or corrupting data — use upserts (MERGE), deterministic partition overwrites, and natural/composite keys instead of blind INSERTs.\n- Delivery semantics: understand at-least-once vs exactly-once vs at-most-once.\n  - At-least-once + idempotent writes is usually the pragmatic default; true exactly-once (e.g. Kafka transactions) adds real cost/complexity — only pay for it when duplicates are unacceptable (billing, inventory).\n- Schema evolution & data contracts: treat producer→consumer schemas as an API — version them, enforce backward/forward compatibility (Avro/Protobuf + schema registry), and fail loudly on breaking changes rather than let them silently corrupt downstream tables.\n- Data quality & validation at the ingestion boundary: validate row counts, null rates, referential integrity, and value ranges before data lands in production — tools like Great Expectations, dbt tests, or Soda catch bad data before it becomes someone else's incident.\n- DAG design: keep tasks small, single-purpose, and independently retryable; avoid monolithic \"do everything\" tasks — a failed task should never require rerunning the whole pipeline.\n- Backfill strategy: design pipelines to be backfillable from day one — parameterize by execution date/partition, never hardcode \"today,\" and test backfills in a lower environment before running against production history.\n- Dependency management: use explicit data-aware dependencies (sensors/asset dependencies), not just time-based schedules — a downstream job should wait on upstream data availability, not merely \"it's probably done by now.\"\n- Orchestration tool judgment: Airflow (mature, huge ecosystem, task-centric) vs Dagster (asset-centric, strong typing/testing) vs Prefect (lighter-weight, dynamic workflows) — the interview-worthy answer is knowing which tradeoffs justify each choice in a given org.\n- Data modeling for analytics: star schema (fact + dimension tables) for query simplicity and BI-tool compatibility; use slowly changing dimensions (SCD Type 2, effective-dated rows) when history matters, Type 1 (overwrite) when it does not.\n- Partitioning & file layout: partition by the columns queries actually filter on (usually date), keep file sizes in the 100MB-1GB range to avoid the \"small files problem,\" and prefer columnar formats (Parquet/ORC) for analytical workloads.\n- Design for scale and cost, not just correctness: know when to denormalize for read performance, when to pre-aggregate, and when a $50/month scheduled batch job beats a $500/month always-on streaming cluster.\n- Observability: instrument pipelines with row-count/freshness/schema-drift metrics and alerting, not just success/failure logs — a pipeline can \"succeed\" while producing zero rows.\n- Reliability patterns: build in retries with backoff, dead-letter queues for poison records, and circuit breakers for flaky upstream dependencies rather than letting one bad record halt the whole DAG.\n- Interview signal: seniors talk in tradeoffs (\"we chose X because Y constraint, at the cost of Z\"), not tool name-dropping — be ready to justify batch-vs-stream, build-vs-buy, and normalization-vs-denormalization decisions with concrete numbers.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Batch vs streaming: choose based on latency requirements and cost, not novelty — batch (hourly/daily) is simpler, cheaper, easier to debug; streaming (Kafka/Flink) only when sub-minute freshness is a real business requirement.\n- Idempotent pipeline design: every task should be safely re-runnable without duplicating or corrupting data — use upserts (MERGE), deterministic partition overwrites, and natural/composite keys instead of blind INSERTs.\n- Delivery semantics: understand at-least-once vs exactly-once vs at-most-once.\n  - At-least-once + idempotent writes is usually the pragmatic default; true exactly-once (e.g. Kafka transactions) adds real cost/complexity — only pay for it when duplicates are unacceptable (billing, inventory).\n- Schema evolution & data contracts: treat producer→consumer schemas as an API — version them, enforce backward/forward compatibility (Avro/Protobuf + schema registry), and fail loudly on breaking changes rather than let them silently corrupt downstream tables.\n- Data quality & validation at the ingestion boundary: validate row counts, null rates, referential integrity, and value ranges before data lands in production — tools like Great Expectations, dbt tests, or Soda catch bad data before it becomes someone else's incident.\n- DAG design: keep tasks small, single-purpose, and independently retryable; avoid monolithic \"do everything\" tasks — a failed task should never require rerunning the whole pipeline.\n- Backfill strategy: design pipelines to be backfillable from day one — parameterize by execution date/partition, never hardcode \"today,\" and test backfills in a lower environment before running against production history.\n- Dependency management: use explicit data-aware dependencies (sensors/asset dependencies), not just time-based schedules — a downstream job should wait on upstream data availability, not merely \"it's probably done by now.\"\n- Orchestration tool judgment: Airflow (mature, huge ecosystem, task-centric) vs Dagster (asset-centric, strong typing/testing) vs Prefect (lighter-weight, dynamic workflows) — the interview-worthy answer is knowing which tradeoffs justify each choice in a given org.\n- Data modeling for analytics: star schema (fact + dimension tables) for query simplicity and BI-tool compatibility; use slowly changing dimensions (SCD Type 2, effective-dated rows) when history matters, Type 1 (overwrite) when it does not.\n- Partitioning & file layout: partition by the columns queries actually filter on (usually date), keep file sizes in the 100MB-1GB range to avoid the \"small files problem,\" and prefer columnar formats (Parquet/ORC) for analytical workloads.\n- Design for scale and cost, not just correctness: know when to denormalize for read performance, when to pre-aggregate, and when a $50/month scheduled batch job beats a $500/month always-on streaming cluster.\n- Observability: instrument pipelines with row-count/freshness/schema-drift metrics and alerting, not just success/failure logs — a pipeline can \"succeed\" while producing zero rows.\n- Reliability patterns: build in retries with backoff, dead-letter queues for poison records, and circuit breakers for flaky upstream dependencies rather than letting one bad record halt the whole DAG.\n- Interview signal: seniors talk in tradeoffs (\"we chose X because Y constraint, at the cost of Z\"), not tool name-dropping — be ready to justify batch-vs-stream, build-vs-buy, and normalization-vs-denormalization decisions with concrete numbers.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
